  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA of length N+1N+1N+1, denoted [A1,A2,…,AN+1][A_1, A_2,\\ldots, A_{N+1}][A1​,A2​,…,AN+1​].\n\nYou can perform the following operation on the array AAA:\n\n\nChoose an index iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N) such that Ai≤2⋅AN+1A_i \\leq 2\\cdot A_{N+1}Ai​≤2⋅AN+1​.\n\nThen, swap the values of AiA_iAi​ and AN+1A_{N+1}AN+1​.\nThat is, you can swap the element at index N+1N+1N+1 with some element of the array that's at most twice as large as it.\n\nYou may perform this operation as many times as you like (even zero times).\n\nFind the **minimum** possible sum of **the first NNN elements** of AAA after the operations, i.e, the minimum possible value of\n\nA1+A2+…+ANA_1 + A_2 + \\ldots + A_NA1​+A2​+…+AN​\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — meaning the length of the array is N+1N+1N+1.\nThe second line contains N+1N+1N+1 space-separated integers A1,A2,…,AN,AN+1A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_N, A_{N+1}A1​,A2​,…,AN​,AN+1​, the initial values of the array.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum possible sum of AAA after performing some replacement operations.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤2001 \\leq T \\leq 2001≤T≤200",
      "1≤N≤2001 \\leq N \\leq 2001≤N≤200",
      "1≤Ai≤10001 \\leq A_i \\leq 10001≤Ai​≤1000"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n4\n5 2 1 2 3\n3\n3 10 30 1\n4\n16 4 8 2 1\n4\n20 100 30 49 15",
        "output": "8\n43\n15\n165",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Initially, A=[5,2,1,2,3]A = [5, 2, 1, 2, 3]A=[5,2,1,2,3].\nPerform one operation with i=1i = 1i=1, which is allowed since 5=A1≤2⋅AN+1=65 = A_1 \\leq 2\\cdot A_{N+1} = 65=A1​≤2⋅AN+1​=6.\nAfter the swap, A=[3,2,1,2,5]A = [3, 2, 1, 2, 5]A=[3,2,1,2,5].\nThe sum of the first NNN elements is 888, which is the minimum possible.\nTest case 222: Initially, A=[3,10,30,1]A = [3, 10, 30, 1]A=[3,10,30,1]. AN+1A_{N+1}AN+1​ cannot be swapped with anything since it's too small. So, the answer is just the sum of the first NNN elements, which is 3+10+30=433+10+30 = 433+10+30=43.\nTest case 333: Initially, A=[16,4,8,2,1]A = [16, 4, 8, 2, 1]A=[16,4,8,2,1]. Consider the following sequence of operations:\n\nChoose i=4i = 4i=4, which is valid since 2≤2⋅12 \\leq 2\\cdot 12≤2⋅1. Now, A=[16,4,8,1,2]A = [16, 4, 8, 1, 2]A=[16,4,8,1,2].\nChoose i=2i = 2i=2, which is valid since 4≤2⋅24 \\leq 2\\cdot 24≤2⋅2. Now, A=[16,2,8,1,4]A = [16, 2, 8, 1, 4]A=[16,2,8,1,4].\nChoose i=3i = 3i=3, which is valid since 8≤2⋅48 \\leq 2\\cdot 48≤2⋅4. Now, A=[16,2,4,1,8]A = [16, 2, 4, 1, 8]A=[16,2,4,1,8].\nChoose i=1i = 1i=1, which is valid since 16≤2⋅816 \\leq 2\\cdot 816≤2⋅8. Now, A=[8,2,4,1,16]A = [8, 2, 4, 1, 16]A=[8,2,4,1,16].\n\nThe sum of the first NNN elements is 151515 which is the best we can do."
      }
    ]
